<!--
    RE-friction round 12, need `x86-64-syscall-wrapper` (challenge
    652e6f896e7e520ff1c3db3c): a three-argument x86-64 Linux syscall wrapper
    decompiles to

        long kernel_call(long number,long address,long length)
        { syscall(); return number; }

    even with an explicit prototype and every argument-recovery option on.  The
    return value is a false statement about the program: it is the syscall
    NUMBER the caller passed in, not the syscall's result.

    Root cause (P2, flow classification).  ia.sinc:4836 lowers the instruction as
    `RCX = inst_next; R11 = rflags; syscall();` - a CALLOTHER with NO inputs and
    NO output - and that one fact produces both halves.  No inputs: the register
    writes that set the call up have no reader, the dead-code fixpoint collects
    them, and the op prints with empty parens.  No output: nothing redefines RAX
    across the instruction, so the RAX stored afterwards is still the one loaded
    from `number`, and copy propagation is right to return it.

    `linuxsyscall` does not cover this.  There is no CALLIND to retarget (a bare
    CALLOTHER, not the `int 0x80` `intloc = swi(0x80); call [intloc]` pair), the
    number is a runtime parameter rather than a constant it could look up, and it
    is gated to x86-32.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 "flow-classification".  The P0 assertion
    `option x64syscall off|on|abi` decides what register effects the user-op
    carries.

    Three functions, one per question the option answers:

        kernel_call   the witness, all setup in the syscall's own block
        plain_add     the control, no SYSCALL at all
        split_setup   RDI is set two blocks back, RAX in the immediate preds

    Pass 1 (`off`) reproduces the bug.  Pass 2 (`on`) takes only the registers
    the block provably sets up, so kernel_call is whole and split_setup keeps RAX
    alone.  Pass 3 (`abi`) takes all six unconditionally, which recovers
    split_setup's RDI at the price of three registers nothing writes.
-->
